answer=A programmer can do this by writing a **template program** with **holes** or **template parameters** that mark the missing parts.

The TACAS paper says:

> “A synthesis template expresses the shape of program constructs, but includes holes (sometimes called template parameters)... We refer to a program containing such templates as a template program”

It also explains that synthesis systems can then **fill in well-structured gaps**:

> “many practical synthesis techniques operate on partially-complete programs, filling in well-structured gaps”

So the idea is to leave placeholders in the code, and let a program synthesis tool instantiate those placeholders automatically.
